import{_ as o,C as n,o as i,c as s,aB as t,E as r}from"./chunks/framework.BPloFSFy.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Core leaf gas exchange","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"core_exchange.md","filePath":"core_exchange.md","lastUpdated":null}'),l={name:"core_exchange.md"};function c(d,e,h,p,u,m){const a=n("LeafCharts");return i(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t('<h1 id="Core-leaf-gas-exchange" tabindex="-1">Core leaf gas exchange <a class="header-anchor" href="#Core-leaf-gas-exchange" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Core leaf gas exchange {#Core-leaf-gas-exchange}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>LeafModel</code> holds three concrete components under <code>AbstractPhotosynthesis</code>, <code>AbstractStomatalModel</code>, and <code>AbstractOxygenExchange</code>. State belongs to the caller. Arrays, device selection, time integration, canopy transport, and soils are external. See <a href="https://github.com/RSFraLab/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/blob/main/examples/core_exchange.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the runnable example</a> and its <a href="https://github.com/RSFraLab/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/blob/main/examples/parameters/core_exchange.toml" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">explicit settings</a>.</p><p>The short equilibrium script calls the production solver, oxygen budget, and sensitivities. TOML parsing and parameter construction live in <code>examples/setup/</code>. For time evolution, run <a href="https://github.com/RSFraLab/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/blob/main/examples/stomatal_response.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">stomatal_response.jl</a>.</p><h2 id="Worked-light-to-dark-example" tabindex="-1">Worked light-to-dark example <a class="header-anchor" href="#Worked-light-to-dark-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Worked light-to-dark example {#Worked-light-to-dark-example}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="leaf-app" data-leaf-app="figures" data-figures="conductance gas-exchange"></div>',5)),r(a),e[1]||(e[1]=t('<p>These plots use the illustrative TOML settings: 1800 s in light followed by 1800 s of darkness, opening/closing timescales of 600/300 s, and a 1 s Euler step. Dashed equilibrium curves are fully coupled solves for each forcing interval; they are distinct from the instantaneous target driving the prognostic tendency. The state is continuous at the light switch; fluxes may change immediately. Net O₂ and CO₂ overlap for the chosen core stoichiometry and respiratory quotient of one, not as a general constraint on all leaves. Leaf temperature and humidity are held fixed. This is not an observed time-course fit or a Bonan 12.1 reproduction.</p><p>Each plot offers FP32/FP64 selection, data inspection and downloads; the <a href="/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/dev/examples.html#gallery">example gallery</a> shows all examples together. <a href="/LeafPhotosynthesis.jl/dev/examples.html#gallery-reproduce">Regeneration instructions</a>.</p><h2 id="Stomatal-targets-and-evolution" tabindex="-1">Stomatal targets and evolution <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stomatal-targets-and-evolution" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stomatal targets and evolution {#Stomatal-targets-and-evolution}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>BallBerry</code> and <code>Medlyn</code> implement <code>AbstractStomatalTarget</code>. Their inputs are net assimilation, leaf-surface CO₂, pressure, and prescribed surface humidity (Ball–Berry) or VPD (Medlyn); each law checks only the humidity driver it reads, and the other may be <code>NaN</code>. Conductance is mol H₂O m⁻² leaf s⁻¹. The Medlyn slope uses Pa^½, not kPa^½. The Medlyn H₂O/CO₂ ratio is not a parameter of <code>Medlyn</code>: <code>target_conductance(target, drivers, diffusion)</code> reads it from the leaf&#39;s <code>SeriesDiffusion</code>, so the stomatal law and the diffusion path cannot disagree. Invalid target parameters throw an <code>ArgumentError</code> when the target is constructed (they are fixed parameters, set once on the host); invalid drivers give <code>NaN</code>. Each law has an explicit minimum conductance; negative assimilation contributes zero to its light-dependent opening term without clipping the carbon flux itself.</p><p><code>SteadyStateStomata(target)</code> evaluates an instantaneous target. It does not by itself solve the coupled equilibrium of assimilation, diffusion, and stomatal conductance. Call <code>equilibrium_gas_exchange</code> for that closure, with an explicit <code>EquilibriumSolver</code>. It uses safeguarded Newton or reference bisection and reports convergence, iterations, and pressure/flux residuals. It supports minimum or quadratic limitation and signed or clipped assimilation. See <a href="./equilibrium_and_sensitivities.html">equations and sensitivities</a>.</p><p><code>PrognosticStomata</code> exposes <code>(target - conductance) / timescale</code>. Opening and closing timescales are independent parameters in seconds. This follows the first-order framework in <a href="https://bg.copernicus.org/articles/21/1501/2024/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Liu et al. (2024), Eq. 3</a>. The tendency does not advance or mutate <code>StomatalState</code>. The caller&#39;s integrator must resolve the dynamics and target feedback; the example&#39;s Euler loop is illustrative.</p><p><code>NoStomatalDynamics</code> skips target-law evaluation and returns zero tendency, retaining the caller&#39;s prescribed conductance. It does not remove stomatal resistance.</p><h2 id="Analytical-diffusion-at-known-conductance" tabindex="-1">Analytical diffusion at known conductance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Analytical-diffusion-at-known-conductance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Analytical diffusion at known conductance {#Analytical-diffusion-at-known-conductance}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>SeriesDiffusion</code> specifies boundary-layer/mesophyll CO₂ conductances and the H₂O/CO₂ stomatal diffusivity ratio, the package&#39;s only copy of that ratio. <code>gas_exchange</code> combines these resistances, then solves the Rubisco and electron-limited supply–demand quadratics analytically. Its supported biochemical choice is <code>MinimumLimitation</code> with <code>SignedAssimilation</code>. Smooth co-limitation is supported in the iterative equilibrium solver, not silently approximated by this analytical prescribed-conductance closure.</p><p>The reported CO₂ sites are distinct: leaf surface, intercellular space, and biochemical site. Finite mesophyll resistance makes Ci different from Cc. The returned diffusion residual compares supply and demand; tests also compare the analytic root with an independent high-precision bracketed solution. Sub-compensation conditions retain signed fluxes. Closed stomata (<code>gs = 0</code>) are supported where a steady state exists: in light the sealed leaf reaches the CO₂ compensation point of the model (net assimilation zero, respiration included), returned analytically. Only a leaf that respires on balance (darkness, or light below the light compensation point) would need CO₂ storage; there no steady state exists and every field is <code>NaN</code>.</p><p><code>leaf_tendencies</code> assembles gas exchange, the current stomatal target/tendency, and optional oxygen diagnostics. It implements the prescribed-conductance path needed for prognostic stomata without an A–Ci fixed-point iteration. Humidity, VPD, leaf temperature, and boundary-layer conductance are prescribed in this first core; there is no humidity or energy-balance solve hidden inside it.</p><h2 id="Oxygen-budget-and-explicit-disabling" tabindex="-1">Oxygen budget and explicit disabling <a class="header-anchor" href="#Oxygen-budget-and-explicit-disabling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Oxygen budget and explicit disabling {#Oxygen-budget-and-explicit-disabling}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>CoreOxygenExchange</code> separates PSII gross production, photorespiratory uptake, respiratory uptake, and net release. Its low-level input (<code>OxygenFluxInputs</code>) is realized Rubisco carboxylation, Rubisco oxygenation, and respiratory CO₂ release, the same fluxes the coupled method takes from the photosynthesis model, so both give the same budget for the same fluxes. All fluxes are per leaf area; net O₂ release is positive, whereas net CO₂ uptake is positive in photosynthesis. The respiratory quotient is CO₂ released per O₂ consumed. Stoichiometric values are explicit parameters; see <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5933133/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Gauthier et al. (2018)</a>.</p><p>Both methods compute the budget from carboxylation and oxygenation: gross O₂ evolution is <code>vc + (o_po − c_po) vo</code> (<code>vc + vo</code> for the canonical 1.5 and 0.5, two NADPH per carboxylation and per oxygenation), independent of the electron stoichiometry of the photosynthesis model. Electrons beyond that NADPH demand (an ATP-limited FvCB stoichiometry such as <code>e_c = 4.5</code>) are taken to return to O₂ without net O₂ exchange. It derives realized turnover from the limiting biochemical capacity, not from potential electron transport. This retains nonzero gross fluxes at the compensation point without dividing by a vanishing net assimilation rate. With RQ = 1, net O₂ release equals net CO₂ uptake for every valid electron stoichiometry; the tests check this identity and the separate gross terms. Changing RQ changes that relation.</p><p>The adapter requires signed assimilation. A clipped sub-compensation curve cannot provide the same carbon/oxygen budget. Gamma-star must match the prescribed O₂ level; a temperature response alone does not provide its oxygen dependence. Alternative electron sinks, cyclic flow, oxygen-limited respiration, and internal O₂ transport/storage are outside this initial core.</p><p><code>NoOxygenExchange</code> returns <code>nothing</code>, indicating an uncomputed diagnostic rather than a physically zero flux. Dispatch omits the oxygen calculation entirely. It is the default oxygen component; the user enables the budget explicitly.</p>',16))])}const y=o(l,[["render",c]]);export{f as __pageData,y as default};
